Notice that <imath>\overline {AD}</imath> (not shown) and <imath>\overline {BC}</imath> are parallel. <imath>\overline {XY}</imath> divides transversals <imath>\overline {AB}</imath> and <imath>\overline {CD}</imath> into a <imath>1:1</imath> ratio (This can be shown by similar triangles.). Thus, it must also divide transversal <imath>\overline {AC}</imath> and transversal <imath>\overline {CO}</imath> into a <imath>1:1</imath> ratio. By symmetry, the same applies for <imath>\overline {CE}</imath> and <imath>\overline {EA}</imath> as well as <imath>\overline {EM}</imath> and <imath>\overline {AN}.</imath>

In <imath>\triangle ACE,</imath> we see that <imath>\frac{[MNO]}{[ACE]} = \frac{1}{4}</imath> and <imath>\frac{[MPN]}{[ACE]} = \frac{1}{8}.</imath> Our desired area becomes <cmath>\left(\frac{1}{4}+3 \cdot \frac{1}{8}\right) \cdot \frac{(\sqrt{3})^2 \cdot \sqrt{3}}{4} = \boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ \frac {15}{32}\sqrt{3}}.</cmath>

==Solution 2 (Area Subtraction)==
Instead of directly finding the desired hexagonal area, <imath>\triangle XYZ</imath> can be found. It consists of three triangles and the desired hexagon. Given triangle rotational symmetry, the three triangles are congruent. See that <imath>\triangle XYZ</imath> and <imath>\triangle ACE</imath> are equilateral, so <imath>m\angle PXN=60,</imath> so <imath>m\angle AXP = \frac{180-60}{2}=60.</imath> As <imath>\overline {AC}</imath> is a transversal running through <imath>\overline {FC}</imath> (use your imagination) and <imath>\overline {AB},</imath> we have <imath>m\angle BAC=m\angle FCA = \frac{m\angle ACE}{2}=30.</imath> 

Then, <imath>\triangle APX</imath> is a <imath>30</imath>-<imath>60</imath>-<imath>90</imath> triangle. By HL congruence, <imath>\triangle APX \cong \triangle NPX.</imath> Note that <imath>AX=\frac{1}{2}.</imath> Then, the area of <imath>\triangle PXN</imath> is <imath>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{32}.</imath> There are three such triangles for a total area of <imath>\triangle XYZ</imath> is <imath>\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{32}.</imath> Find the side of <imath>\triangle XYZ</imath> to be <imath>\frac{3}{2},</imath> so the area is <imath>\frac{9\sqrt{3}}{16}.</imath> 

The answer is <cmath>\frac{9\sqrt{3}}{16}-\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{32}=\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ \frac {15}{32}\sqrt{3}}.</cmath>
~BJHHar

==Solution 3 (Area Subtraction)==
We will find its area by subtracting the three triangles congruent to triangle APM from <imath>\triangle ACE</imath>. By the rotational symmetry of the regular hexagon, the three corner triangles outside the intersection (<imath>\triangle APM</imath>, <imath>\triangle CQN</imath>, and <imath>\triangle ERO</imath>) are all congruent. 

We first find the area of the large equilateral triangle <imath>\triangle ACE</imath>. Since the regular hexagon has a side length of <imath>1</imath>, the side length of <imath>\triangle ACE</imath> is <imath>\sqrt{3}.</imath> Using the area formula for an equilateral triangle, we have:
<cmath>[\text{ACE}] = \frac{(\sqrt{3})^2\sqrt{3}}{4} = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{4}.</cmath>

To find the area of the corner triangle <imath>\triangle APM</imath>, we can compare it to the smaller adjacent triangle <imath>\triangle ZMR.</imath> Since <imath>\triangle APM</imath> and <imath>\triangle ZMR</imath> share the same vertical angles at vertex <imath>M</imath> and are bounded by the grid lines of the hexagon, analyzing their bases and heights reveals that the area of <imath>\triangle APM</imath> is exactly <imath>3</imath> times the area of <imath>\triangle ZMR.</imath> Thus, the area ratio between <imath>\triangle APM</imath> and <imath>\triangle ZMR</imath> is <imath>3:1.</imath> 

The area of the smaller triangle <imath>\triangle ZMR</imath> can be calculated from the grid as:
<cmath>[\text{ZMR}] = \frac{1}{4} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{32}.</cmath>

Multiplying this by <imath>3</imath> gives the true area of one corner triangle <imath>\triangle APM</imath>:
<cmath>[\text{APM}] = 3 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{32} = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{32}.</cmath>

Since there are three such identical corner triangles surrounding the inner hexagon, we subtract their total area from the area of <imath>\triangle ACE</imath>:

The answer is <cmath>\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{4} - 3\left(\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{32}\right) = \frac{24\sqrt{3}}{32} - \frac{9\sqrt{3}}{32} = \boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ \frac {15}{32}\sqrt{3}}.</cmath>

~viviviiccotrrr1098

